From adce8d1dcd39d413b4798ca0c55b7e842f6c2d06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 04:35:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(guards): 37 of 54 guards never ran in CI; pnpm verify
 stopped at the first failure
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Measured on floor-graph: package.json defines 54 gating guards, the CI Guards
job ran 17, and `pnpm verify` was a 57-link && chain that stopped at link 3.
Thirty-seven guards had never gated a merge. Nobody disabled them — they were
added to package.json and the second edit, to the workflow, was forgotten, which
is invisible because a step never written cannot appear as missing.

One of the 37 was verify:destinations, and it was failing on a real defect that
had already shipped: /hardwood-stairs-toronto links to
/projects/maple-vaughan-curved-stair, and MEDIA-01 added that dynamic route
without registering /projects in the guard's manifest map, so the guard could not
prove the link resolves. Fixed by teaching it to read slugs from a content
directory and registering both /projects and /equipment. Regression-tested with a
planted slug.

pnpm verify is now scripts/verify-all.mjs: the run set is derived from
package.json so no guard can be left out, an unclassified script fails the
runner, all 57 run in parallel in ~3s instead of stopping at the first failure,
and the failures are reprinted in full as the last thing on the screen. That last
part is the point — twice this week a red verify was followed by a commit and a
push, because the next pasted command printed four hundred lines of green on top
of the failure.

The workflow keeps its named steps and gains one final step that runs the whole
set, so CI and a developer's machine can no longer disagree about which guards
exist.

verify:hygiene also stopped guessing. It asserted that every file it flagged was
tracked and printed `git rm --cached … && rm -f …`; on a branch where the files
were not tracked, git exited 1, the && stopped the chain, the rm never ran, and
the next run printed the identical advice. It now asks git, prints the right
command for each case, and says when a file is untracked here but tracked on main
and will therefore return with every checkout.

docs/VERIFY.md has the measurement and how to add a guard.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
